WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.300
How deeply does a protein sit in a membrane, and which way does it face?

2
00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:07.620
Some proteins cling to one surface.

3
00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:12.340
Others pierce the oily core, with ends on opposite sides.

4
00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:16.380
Watching an intact membrane cannot tell them apart.

5
00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.180
Two paired assays test burial and sidedness.

6
00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:24.630
First, test burial.

7
00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:30.770
Wash membranes with high salt, or with high pH, called alkaline extraction.

8
00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:37.590
Salt and high pH loosen proteins held only by contacts at the membrane's face.

9
00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:42.130
Surface-clinging peripheral proteins come off into the liquid.

10
00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:48.250
Core-buried integral proteins stay behind when membranes are spun down into a

11
00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:53.830
pellet. Only soap-like detergent, which dissolves the bilayer, releases them.

12
00:00:54.833 --> 00:00:59.793
Once a protein stays in the core, ask its topology: which end faces each side?

13
00:01:01.073 --> 00:01:06.653
Break cells, and ER membranes reseal into sealed microsomes with their lumen inside.

14
00:01:07.993 --> 00:01:13.153
Put distinct antibody tags on both ends, then add protein-cutting protease outside.

15
00:01:14.613 --> 00:01:16.673
Protease cannot cross a sealed bilayer.

16
00:01:16.673 --> 00:01:21.973
It removes the cytosolic tag, leaving the lumenal tag intact.

17
00:01:23.167 --> 00:01:25.067
Pause and test that conclusion.

18
00:01:26.787 --> 00:01:30.007
Suppose one tag survives protease treatment on your gel.

19
00:01:31.247 --> 00:01:34.627
Does survival alone prove that tag sat inside the lumen?

20
00:01:35.887 --> 00:01:38.927
What else could protect it, and what control rules that out?

21
00:01:44.417 --> 00:01:47.357
No: the tag itself might resist the protease.

22
00:01:48.677 --> 00:01:50.797
Add detergent first to open the vesicles.

23
00:01:52.057 --> 00:01:55.637
If both tags vanish, the sealed membrane did the protecting.

24
00:01:57.177 --> 00:02:01.637
Washes test burial, while protease with and without detergent proves each side.

25
00:02:02.917 --> 00:02:08.437
One extraction catch: lipid-anchored proteins can also resist washes, since their

26
00:02:08.437 --> 00:02:10.197
lipid tail sits in the bilayer.

27
00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:13.150
A second test uses sugars.

28
00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:17.850
Cells add N-linked sugars only inside the ER lumen.

29
00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:21.630
A sugar makes the protein run higher on a gel.

30
00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:27.790
If endoglycosidase H removes that shift, the end carrying the site reached the

31
00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:33.550
lumen. Sites closer than 12 to 14 residues to the membrane go unused.

32
00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:38.150
So a missing sugar does not by itself prove the cytosol.

33
00:02:39.233 --> 00:02:42.233
These assays unlocked how cells insert proteins.

34
00:02:43.693 --> 00:02:50.113
In 1975, Günter Blobel and Bernhard Dobberstein applied protease protection to

35
00:02:50.113 --> 00:02:51.713
dog-pancreas microsomes.

36
00:02:53.053 --> 00:02:58.073
A mouse antibody light chain was trimmed and hidden inside, but only if membranes

37
00:02:58.073 --> 00:02:59.393
were present during synthesis.

38
00:03:00.793 --> 00:03:05.653
In 1981, Peter Walter and Blobel washed those membranes in high salt.

39
00:03:06.653 --> 00:03:12.453
Selective ribosome binding vanished until purified signal recognition particle

40
00:03:12.453 --> 00:03:13.453
restored it.

41
00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:20.920
In 1999, Goder, Bieri, and Spiess combined sugar mapping and alkaline extraction in

42
00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:28.180
COS-1 cells. One added sugar site cut crossing by the chain's tail end, its C

43
00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:31.000
terminus, from about 75% to 30%.

44
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:36.900
percent. They concluded that growing chains can reorient inside the entry channel.

45
00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:41.940
Strip the surface, open the seal, and the parts reveal their sides.
